{:id :h2-update-rollback
 :date "2026-10-25"
 :row :H2
 :claim "Rollback after a bad update: an update that fails to boot is replaced by the previous version automatically."
 :host {:model "MacBookPro18,4", :cpu "Apple M1 Max", :os "Darwin 25.4.0 arm64"}
 :commit "7bcfbd73a0984b8c0af90c5e11bd7245dbc1042d"
 :verdicts {"bad-update-applied" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "boot A: AIUEOS_UPDATE_APPLIED — a validly signed package carrying a garbage KERNEL.ELF is applied to a persistent disk image"},
            "update-fails-to-boot" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "boot B: AIUEOS_LOADER_RECOVERY_OK kernel-from-alternate-volume — the applied kernel fails the loader's compiled-in digest admission"},
            "rolled-back-automatically" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "AIUEOS_UPDATE_ROLLED_BACK previous-version-restored — the previous boot chain is restored over the system ESP with read-back compare"},
            "previous-version-runs" {:green? true, :reason :marker, :marker "AIUEOS_DESKTOP_LOOP_OK after the rollback: the previous version boots to the desktop"}}
 :run
 {:boot-a "the broken-kernel update package (manifest signed with the release key over the garbage kernel's sha256) applied via AIUEOS_DISK_SNAPSHOT=off onto a persistent work image"
  :boot-b "the same image without the update volume: the loader's kernel admission fails, the alternate-volume fallback admits the previous kernel, and the rollback block restores KERNEL.ELF + INITRD.IMG + BOOTX64.EFI from that volume over the system ESP"
  :mechanism "the fallback that already existed (bounded recovery: any volume may carry an admitted kernel) now also restores the system chain — the rollback needs no new trust decision because admission still requires the identical compiled-in SHA-256"}
 :caveats
 ["'the previous version' is the chain the alternate volume carries (the release build in this test); the restore source is the admitting volume, not a stored pre-update snapshot"
  "the bad update is functionally bad (a garbage kernel that cannot pass admission); an update that boots but misbehaves is not covered — that needs a runtime health flag"
  "the two-boot run needed a writable disk (the harness gained AIUEOS_DISK_SNAPSHOT; the default stays snapshot=on for artifact immutability)"
  "owner may reject: the rollback restores three fixed file names, matching the update package's scope"]
 :green? true}
